1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,440
An NVMe label tells you something about a
storage technology.

2
00:00:04,060 --> 00:00:08,060
It does not, by itself, tell you how fast
your application will

3
00:00:08,060 --> 00:00:10,120
run on a particular VPS plan.

4
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:15,060
Begin with the task. A small static site,
an active database

5
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:17,800
and a build process use storage
differently.

6
00:00:18,260 --> 00:00:22,660
Ask which operations are actually slowing
your own service before turning

7
00:00:22,660 --> 00:00:24,080
a label into a buying rule.

8
00:00:24,840 --> 00:00:29,180
Next, read the plan documentation for
storage capacity and any published

9
00:00:29,630 --> 00:00:34,300
input-output limits. Shared infrastructure,
workload patterns and provider

10
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:36,400
policies can affect the result.

11
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:41,400
If a useful limit is not documented, record
that gap rather

12
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:43,540
than filling it with an estimated
benchmark.

13
00:00:44,340 --> 00:00:47,740
Keep disk capacity separate from throughput
and latency.

14
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:51,400
A larger disk is not the same promise as
faster storage,

15
00:00:51,880 --> 00:00:57,080
and one short synthetic result would not
describe every application even if

16
00:00:57,080 --> 00:00:58,200
a test had been performed.

17
00:00:59,140 --> 00:01:02,700
Our take: compare the stated constraints
and the workload together.

18
00:01:03,080 --> 00:01:07,060
A clear question for support is more useful
than an unsupported ranking

19
00:01:07,060 --> 00:01:09,240
of every plan labeled NVMe.

20
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:12,760
This video explains how to evaluate the
claim.

21
00:01:13,060 --> 00:01:16,880
It does not report a storage benchmark or
guarantee a performance difference

22
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:21,520
between providers. The description links to
the complete storage guide.

23
00:01:21,980 --> 00:01:26,060
Use it to build a shortlist, then verify
the specific plan documentation

24
00:01:27,040 --> 00:01:30,500
and, where appropriate, run a permitted
test for your own workload.
